  "worked_solution": "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=-2$ it is also  continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical tangent at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at this point.",
